  <title>
   <narrative xml:lang="EN">Formalization facilitated for gender-responsive, inclusive and diversified economic growth</narrative>
  </title>
  <description type="1">
   <narrative xml:lang="EN">Informal workers in Mongolia continue to face deep and persistent challenges due to the high rate of informality of the country, 44% of total employment, including many in agriculture, leaving a large portion of the workforce without formal contracts, social protection, or access to collective bargaining mechanisms.  These workers often remain outside inspection systems and government registries, which makes it difficult for them to benefit from legal protections, enforcement mechanisms, or employment support programmes. There are administrative barriers that currently discourage workers and enterprises from formalizing, for example, high transaction costs, unclear procedures, fragmented documentation requirements, and limited awareness of formalization benefits, factors that particularly affect household based and agricultural self employment.
        Recognizing these systemic vulnerabilities, the ILO is supporting Mongolian tripartite constituents to advance digital formalization pathways that make it easier for informal workers and micro enterprises to enter the formal economy. The Office has already conducted an assessment of existing formalization initiatives and government service processes, identifying key opportunities to establish a national database of self employed workers and to strengthen data exchange protocols across government systems, such as tax, social insurance, access to finance, training, and health services, to enable evidence&#xe2;&#x20ac;&#x2018;based decision&#xe2;&#x20ac;&#x2018;making and more coordinated service delivery.
        Expected change: By strengthening the Government ebusiness platform and building institutional capacity through targeted training and tripartite dialogue, the ILO is enabling a more efficient, transparent, and user friendly administrative environment that reduces registration barriers, supports small and micro enterprises, and accelerates the formalization of informal workers and businesses&#xe3;&#x20ac;&#x201a;</narrative>
  </description>
